Thank you, Jeff, for this very nice introduction.
谢谢杰夫，感谢您如此精彩的介绍。

33
00:16:00,490 --> 00:16:01,870
Is the microphone on.
麦克风打开了吗？

34
00:16:01,870 --> 00:16:03,110
Yes thank you.
是的，谢谢。

35
00:16:03,110 --> 00:16:10,010
And Ronald and I were very happy to be here at Berkeley to give this colloquium.
罗纳德和我非常高兴能来到伯克利参加这次研讨会。

36
00:16:10,010 --> 00:16:15,270
And we without further ado, let me get started.
那么，不耽误时间，我开始吧。

37
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:17,250
Perhaps I'll get a little excited.
也许我会有点激动。

38
00:16:17,250 --> 00:16:20,243
So let me remove my jacket first, just in case.
那我先脱掉外套，以防万一。

39
00:16:20,243 --> 00:16:22,910
Don't think you want to stand in the middle because you'll walk.
别站在中间，你会走动的。

40
00:16:22,910 --> 00:16:25,630
No, no, I will be standing here.
不，不，我会站在这里。

41
00:16:25,630 --> 00:16:33,070
So we want to tell you about EUV lithography.
所以我想跟你们讲讲极紫外光刻技术。

42
00:16:33,070 --> 00:16:37,070
But I want to start with some history.
但我想先从一些历史讲起。

43
00:16:37,070 --> 00:16:40,430
But I'd like to start with the history of photolithography itself.
但我想先讲讲光刻技术本身的历史。

44
00:16:40,430 --> 00:16:42,110
Just give me five minutes.
给我五分钟时间。

45
00:16:42,110 --> 00:16:53,230
Because this may be interesting to know if some of you do not know photolithography or are not familiar with this technology yet, but.
因为如果你们中有些人还不了解光刻技术，或者对这项技术还不熟悉，那这些信息可能就很有意思了。

46
00:16:53,230 --> 00:17:01,970
So let me get started, it's the first part of the talk is the photolithography until early 1980s and then EUV as a next
那我开始讲了，这部分是讲光刻技术直到上世纪80年代初，然后是下一代光刻技术——极紫外光刻技术，以及如何开发极紫外光刻技术以实现大规模生产。

47
00:17:01,970 --> 00:17:10,170
generation technology, lithography technology, and then developing EUV lithography for high volume production.
所以光刻技术本身是在20世纪50年代中期由两组人发明的。

48
00:17:10,170 --> 00:17:17,650
So photolithography itself was invented in the mid 1950s by two groups.
所以光刻技术本身是在20世纪50年代中期由两组人发明的。

49
00:17:17,650 --> 00:17:21,849
First was called Bell Labs where you folks were.
第一个是贝尔实验室，你们就在那里。

50
00:17:21,849 --> 00:17:25,290
And the second group was a US Army's laboratory.
第二个小组是美国陆军的实验室。

51
00:17:25,290 --> 00:17:31,250
So let me focus on the second group because the story was more dramatic.
所以我来重点讲第二个小组，因为这个故事更加戏剧化。

52
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:38,970
In the second group, the goal was to fit a proximity fuse diffuse into a mortar shell.
在第二个小组中，目标是将一个近炸引信装进迫击炮弹壳里。

53
00:17:38,970 --> 00:17:40,590
Mortar shells are not very big.
迫击炮弹壳并不大。

54
00:17:40,590 --> 00:17:44,270
A proximity fuse is basically a cheap radar system.
近炸引信本质上是一个廉价的雷达系统。

55
00:17:44,270 --> 00:17:52,790
OK, the goal is to have the bomb explode in the proximity of the target before hitting the target.
好的，目标是在炸弹击中目标之前，就在目标附近引爆。

56
00:17:52,790 --> 00:17:56,110
To increase the probability of impact.
以提高命中概率。

57
00:17:56,110 --> 00:18:00,010
Because if it explodes right on target, you may miss the target.
因为如果在击中目标的瞬间爆炸，可能会错过目标。

58
00:18:00,010 --> 00:18:04,370
But if you explore the vicinity and your chances are a lot higher hitting the target.
但如果在目标周围引爆，命中目标的几率要高得多。

59
00:18:04,370 --> 00:18:09,050
So this was the goal to put the proximity fuse inside a mortar shell.
所以目标就是把近炸引信放进迫击炮弹壳里。

60
00:18:09,050 --> 00:18:10,990
OK mortar shell is very small.
好的，迫击炮弹壳非常小。

61
00:18:10,990 --> 00:18:16,530
So in those days the transistors were mesa transistors.
当时的晶体管是台面晶体管。

62
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:18,090
Germanium OK.
锗材料可以。

63
00:18:18,090 --> 00:18:23,730
How do you fit a mesa germanium transistor circuit inside a mortar shell.
如何把台面锗晶体管电路装进炮弹壳里？

64
00:18:23,730 --> 00:18:25,210
Well, these two guys.
就是这两个人。

65
00:18:27,910 --> 00:18:32,570
Jay Lathrop and James Knoll from the laboratory,
实验室的杰伊·拉思罗普和詹姆斯·诺尔。

66
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:43,940
they thought of using, of course, they found out that Kodak had a photoresist and they thought of using a microscope, but operating in reverse.
他们想到，当然，他们发现柯达有一种光刻胶，于是想到用显微镜，但反过来操作。

67
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,120
So this is a microscope.
这是一台显微镜。

68
00:18:46,120 --> 00:18:49,820
And this is where the transistor or a piece of germanium is.
晶体管或一块锗材料就在这里。

69
00:18:49,820 --> 00:18:54,080
And they put the photomask here where you would have the Polaroid camera.
他们把光刻掩模放在原本放宝丽来相机的位置。

70
00:18:54,080 --> 00:18:57,260
And you shine white light from the top.
从上方照射白光。

71
00:18:57,260 --> 00:18:58,800
Now what is this filter.
那个滤光片是什么？

72
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:07,540
Well, before the exposure, when you're aligning to the previous layer, you slide the red filter in there so that the photoresist is not exposed.
在曝光前，对齐前一层时，他们会把红色滤光片滑进去，以防止光刻胶被曝光。

73
00:19:07,540 --> 00:19:12,500
Once you do the alignment, you take out the red filter and then the exposure happens.
完成对齐后，你移除红色滤镜，然后进行曝光。

74
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:16,540
You expose this and then you get the transistor.
你对这个进行曝光，之后就得到了晶体管。

75
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:18,640
And the transistor looked like this.
而这个晶体管看起来是这样的。

76
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:23,360
The die is about 1 millimeter to 1 millimeter by 1 millimeter.
芯片尺寸约为1毫米乘1毫米乘1毫米。

77
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:24,360
And this is a mesa.
这是一个台面结构。

78
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:25,960
The base size was not stated.
基区尺寸未说明。

79
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:27,260
The base contact.
基区接触。

80
00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:34,820
This little guy, one of these guys is a base contact is 0.1 by 0.25.
这个小部分，其中一个基区接触的尺寸是0.1乘0.25。

81
00:19:34,820 --> 00:19:36,500
Nowadays it's gigantic.
如今这已经非常巨大了。

82
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,560
But back in those days, it was hard to make.
但在当时，制造起来非常困难。

83
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:43,660
And then they also had to make the emitter contact, which is the other rectangle right here.
然后他们还需要制作发射区接触，也就是这里的另一个矩形。

84
00:19:43,660 --> 00:19:46,080
Same Same dimension.
尺寸相同。

85
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:47,720
It was a success.
这是一次成功。

86
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:53,680
And then what happened was that Jay Lathrop joined ti in 1958.
然后发生的事情是，杰·拉思罗普在1958年加入了德州仪器。

87
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:56,020
And what happened in TI at 19.
德州仪器在1959年发生了什么。

88
00:19:56,020 --> 00:20:00,780
In 1958, Jack Kilby invented the integrated circuit.
1958年，杰克·基尔比发明了集成电路。

89
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:06,920
So Jay Lathrop worked at TI with Jack Kilby for the next 10 years.
于是杰·拉思罗普在接下来的十年里与杰克·基尔比在德州仪器共事。

90
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:10,040
And where did the other guy go.
那个其他人去了哪里。

91
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:12,600
Of course, he went to Fairchild.
当然，他去了仙童公司。

92
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:21,000
1959 right where Robert Noyce and others had invented the integrated circuit there.
1959年，就在罗伯特·诺伊斯和其他人发明集成电路的地方。

93
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,240
So great story, and it's a bit romantic.
所以这是一个精彩的故事，也带点浪漫色彩。

94
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:29,080
So this was in the mid to late 1950s.
这发生在20世纪50年代中期到晚期。

95
00:20:29,080 --> 00:20:31,040
And what happened next.
接下来发生了什么。

96
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:36,330
What happened next is less romantic lithography.
接下来发生的事则没那么浪漫，是光刻技术。

97
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:44,830
In the 60s and the 70, early 70s were mainly contact and proximity printing.
20世纪60年代和70年代，早期主要是接触式和接近式光刻。

98
00:20:44,830 --> 00:20:50,250
Because those days, resolution wasn't really the top issue.
那时候，分辨率并不是最核心的问题。

99
00:20:50,250 --> 00:20:53,250
The top issue was how to get these layers to align.
最核心的问题是如何让这些层对准。

100
00:20:53,250 --> 00:20:57,350
So those machines were called aligners back then.
所以那时候这些机器被称为对准仪。

101
00:20:57,350 --> 00:21:03,910
And this is an aligner from Casper instruments here in the US.
这是一台来自美国卡斯珀仪器公司的对准仪。

102
00:21:03,910 --> 00:21:08,590
These guys were the King back in the 60s OK.
他们在20世纪60年代是行业霸主。

103
00:21:08,590 --> 00:21:11,470
They were very good at doing contact printing.
他们在接触式光刻方面非常擅长。

104
00:21:11,470 --> 00:21:13,690
We put the mask right on top of the wafer.
我们把掩模直接放在晶圆上方。

105
00:21:13,690 --> 00:21:14,570
You're aligned.
你已经对准了。

106
00:21:14,570 --> 00:21:16,350
And then you shine the light.
然后你照射光线。

107
00:21:16,350 --> 00:21:23,070
The problem with contact printing is that it generates particles in the space or.
接触式光刻的问题在于，会在空间中产生颗粒。

108
00:21:23,070 --> 00:21:26,510
Well, you're rubbing the wafer against the mask.
你实际上是在让晶圆与掩模相互摩擦。

109
00:21:26,510 --> 00:21:29,150
So what happened was that later.
后来发生的事情是这样。

110
00:21:29,150 --> 00:21:34,150
Well, maybe we should create a gap between the mask and the wafer.
或许我们应该在掩模和晶圆之间留出一个间隙。

111
00:21:34,150 --> 00:21:38,290
And then they went from contact printing to proximity printing.
然后他们从接触式光刻转向了近距光刻。

112
00:21:38,290 --> 00:21:44,010
Once proximity printing happens, canon took over as the new King.
近距光刻出现后，佳能成为了新的王者。

113
00:21:44,010 --> 00:21:46,350
This guy faded away.
这个人逐渐淡出了。

114
00:21:46,350 --> 00:21:52,290
And then canon made a much better machine for proximity printing.
然后佳能制造出了一台性能更出色的近距光刻机。

115
00:21:52,290 --> 00:21:57,370
So this is canon machine until.
从那时起，佳能的机器一直占据主导地位。

116
00:21:57,370 --> 00:22:03,130
Well, until proximity printing was superseded by projection imaging.
直到近距光刻被投影成像技术取代。

117
00:22:03,130 --> 00:22:06,610
And this happened in the early 70s.
这一转变发生在上世纪70年代初。

118
00:22:06,610 --> 00:22:09,490
So let me first give an example.
让我先举一个例子。

119
00:22:09,490 --> 00:22:12,450
Why did we go for projection imaging.
为什么我们要转向投影成像技术。

120
00:22:12,450 --> 00:22:23,730
So the example is let's shine a lambda equal to 400 nanometer UV light through a mask.
举个例子，让我们用波长为400纳米的紫外光透过掩模。

121
00:22:23,730 --> 00:22:28,510
Imaging a slit slit is 4 micron wide slit right.
一个4微米宽的狭缝，想象一下就是这样。

122
00:22:28,510 --> 00:22:33,750
And the gap is 1 micron 5 micron and 10 microns.
而间隙分别是1微米、5微米和10微米。

123
00:22:33,750 --> 00:22:37,010
And this is the kind of aerial image that you get.
这就是你所得到的这种空中图像。

124
00:22:37,010 --> 00:22:45,470
As you can see that this is almost at the limit of resolution Of proximity printing.
你可以看到，这几乎已经达到了邻近印刷分辨率的极限。

125
00:22:45,470 --> 00:22:46,330
Because why.
因为原因在于。

126
00:22:46,330 --> 00:22:48,690
Because you really cannot control the gap very well.
因为你真的无法很好地控制间隙。

127
00:22:48,690 --> 00:22:51,910
If you control the gap perfectly then it's fine.
如果你能完美控制间隙，那就没问题。

128
00:22:51,910 --> 00:22:57,090
But you can't because first the control itself mechanism itself wasn't very good.
但你做不到，首先是因为控制机制本身就不够好。

129
00:22:57,090 --> 00:23:01,990
But also the wafer has is not flat.
而且晶圆本身也不平整。

130
00:23:01,990 --> 00:23:03,070
It's warped.
它是弯曲的。

131
00:23:03,070 --> 00:23:08,030
So it's much better if you do it through a lens.
所以通过透镜来实现要好得多。

132
00:23:08,030 --> 00:23:15,890
And this is projection printing same wavelengths of light with a lens that is not very sophisticated.
这就是投影印刷，使用相同波长的光，通过一个并不复杂的透镜。

133
00:23:15,890 --> 00:23:19,390
0.2 a very low numerical aperture lens.
0.2，一个非常低的数值孔径镜头。

134
00:23:19,390 --> 00:23:21,890
And this is what you get if you're unfocused.
这就是你对焦不准时得到的结果。

135
00:23:21,890 --> 00:23:23,410
You're looking at the red curve.
你看到的是红色曲线。

136
00:23:23,410 --> 00:23:29,780
And if you're off by plus 5 plus minus 5 microns you're looking at the blue curve.
如果你偏离了正负5微米，你看到的就是蓝色曲线。

137
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:34,980
So in one case you're fighting diffraction.
在一种情况下，你在对抗衍射。

138
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:38,420
The other case, you're taking advantage of diffraction.
